body:
Agent-guide layout composition: both author guides — the deck guide (agent-authoring) and the library guide (library-agent-guide) — document `{{ template }}` layout composition, so an agent authoring a library can compose layouts without reading Go source. The guide states:
- a library layout's name is its layer-relative forward-slash path — `slides/<template-name>/layout.html.tmpl` for a slide layout and `sections/<template-name>/layout.html.tmpl` for a section layout — the same string on Windows, macOS and Linux and wherever the layer sits on disk, never a layer root, a `templates/` prefix, a drive letter, a backslash or an absolute path, and not the template name;
- a layout composes another library layout with the standard `{{ template "<layout name>" . }}` action;
- a layout may include layouts of its own layer;
- in a two-layer library a local-layer layout may include an external-layer layout by that layout's own layer-relative name;
- an external-layer layout naming a local-layer layout is a templates error, because the external layer validates standalone;
- an included layout executes in the invoking layout's namespace, so its `{{ media }}` helper binds to the invoking template's own layer, while a `section` helper call rebinds the namespace to the target's own layer.

The guide rules are the readable counterpart of the implemented load-time behaviour (example-layout-composition, library-layer-references, templates-dir-validation step 6a). A build/test-time self-test carries the composition tokens for both guides (agent-guide-drift-check).

AC:
- both guides name the layer-relative layout path form and document the `{{ template }}` action;
- both guides state the own-layer, local-to-external (allowed) and external-to-local (templates error) rules;
- both guides state that an included layout executes in the invoking layout's namespace and that a `section` call rebinds to the target's layer;
- the drift self-test (agent-guide-drift-check) requires the composition tokens in each guide that owns them.
